<section class="build-section">
<div class="shell build-grid reveal">
<div><p class="kicker">Engineering process</p><h2>Build, test, and iterate.</h2><p>I approach projects by breaking larger systems into smaller, testable parts. I validate the hardware and software independently, identify failure points, and then iterate on the wiring, code, or mechanism until the full system behaves reliably.</p></div>
<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><div class="step-num">01</div><div><h3>Sense</h3><p>Start by understanding the input and validating that the sensor data is consistent enough to use.</p></div></div>
<div class="step"><div class="step-num">02</div><div><h3>Decide</h3><p>Translate the input into clear control logic that defines how the system should respond.</p></div></div>
<div class="step"><div class="step-num">03</div><div><h3>Act</h3><p>Use actuators and feedback devices—such as servos, LEDs, buzzers, and displays—to produce a clear physical response.</p></div></div>
<div class="step"><div class="step-num">04</div><div><h3>Iterate</h3><p>I test the complete system under real conditions, identify weak points, and refine the hardware or software until the behavior is more consistent and dependable.</p></div></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="about shell" id="about">
<div class="about-grid reveal">
<div><p class="kicker">About</p><h2>Building a stronger engineering foundation through projects.</h2></div>
<div class="about-copy"><p>I’m especially interested in robotics, mechanical design, and intelligent physical systems—particularly the point where software, sensing, and mechanical hardware interact.</p><p>I began with an ELEGOO Mega 2560 kit to develop a practical foundation in electronics and embedded systems. Since then, I’ve progressed from individual sensor exercises to projects that combine sensing, control logic, user feedback, and actuation. Alongside mechanical engineering, I’m studying data science because I want to understand both physical systems and the data they generate. I’m continuing to build because each project gives me a clearer sense of what I know, what I still need to learn, and where I want to grow as an engineer.</p><div class="tool-list"><span>Arduino Mega</span><span>Ultrasonic sensing</span><span>Servo control</span><span>MAX7219</span><span>Keypads</span><span>Joysticks</span><span>Serial debugging</span><span>Rapid prototyping</span></div></div>
</div>
</section>
