<section aria-label="Smart Safe case study" class="case-modal" id="smart-safe-case">
<a aria-label="Close case study" class="case-backdrop" href="#work"></a>
<div class="case-sheet">
<div class="case-topbar">
<p>01 / 05</p>
<a aria-label="Close" class="close-case" href="#work">×</a>
</div>
<div class="case-hero">
<div>
<p class="kicker">Embedded systems · access control</p>
<h2>Smart Safe</h2>
<p class="case-lede">I built a keypad-controlled safe that combines a servo lock with LED-matrix, RGB, and buzzer feedback, plus clap-triggered access.</p>
<div class="tag-row light"><span>Arduino Mega</span><span>4×4 Keypad</span><span>Servo</span><span>MAX7219</span><span>Buzzer</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Frame from Smart Safe video" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="image-02.png"/>
</div>
<div class="case-grid">
<div><p class="mini-label">The challenge</p><p>The main challenge was coordinating several inputs and outputs while keeping the system’s state clear to the user through visual and audio feedback.</p></div>
<div><p class="mini-label">What I learned</p><p>This project was my first experience integrating several modules into one coordinated system. It strengthened my understanding of state-driven logic and showed me the importance of designing clear user feedback, not just making individual components function.</p></div>
<div class="case-built"><p class="mini-label">What I built</p><ol><li>Password entry through a 4×4 keypad</li><li>Servo-driven physical locking</li><li>Color and buzzer feedback based on code accuracy</li><li>LED-matrix status display</li><li>Clap detection as an alternate input</li></ol></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section aria-label="8×8 Pong vs. AI case study" class="case-modal" id="pong-case">
<a aria-label="Close case study" class="case-backdrop" href="#work"></a>
<div class="case-sheet">
<div class="case-topbar">
<p>02 / 05</p>
<a aria-label="Close" class="close-case" href="#work">×</a>
</div>
<div class="case-hero">
<div>
<p class="kicker">Game logic · human-machine interaction</p>
<h2>8×8 Pong vs. AI</h2>
<p class="case-lede">I turned an 8×8 LED matrix into a playable Pong game with joystick control, three AI difficulty levels, scoring, and win/loss screens.</p>
<div class="tag-row light"><span>MAX7219</span><span>Joystick</span><span>State Machines</span><span>AI Opponent</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Frame from 8×8 Pong vs. AI video" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="image-01.png"/>
</div>
<div class="case-grid">
<div><p class="mini-label">The challenge</p><p>The challenge was fitting responsive gameplay, scoring, difficulty selection, and changing ball speed into a display with only 64 pixels.</p></div>
<div><p class="mini-label">What I learned</p><p>This project strengthened my understanding of state machines, collision logic, and debugging interactions between user input, display output, and game behavior. It also showed me how strongly timing decisions affect responsiveness and playability.</p></div>
<div class="case-built"><p class="mini-label">What I built</p><ol><li>Analog joystick control for the player paddle</li><li>Easy, Medium, and Hard AI modes</li><li>Five-point win condition</li><li>Dedicated win/loss and difficulty screens</li><li>Ball-speed progression and collision logic</li></ol></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section aria-label="Reaction-Time Game case study" class="case-modal" id="reaction-time-case">
<a aria-label="Close case study" class="case-backdrop" href="#work"></a>
<div class="case-sheet">
<div class="case-topbar">
<p>03 / 05</p>
<a aria-label="Close" class="close-case" href="#work">×</a>
</div>
<div class="case-hero">
<div>
<p class="kicker">Timing · interactive electronics</p>
<h2>Reaction-Time Game</h2>
<p class="case-lede">I built a reflex and sequence game that measures reaction time and keeps track of score, lives, false starts, and performance in the Serial Monitor.</p>
<div class="tag-row light"><span>Keypad</span><span>LEDs</span><span>Timing</span><span>Serial Monitor</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Frame from Reaction-Time Game video" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="image-04.png"/>
</div>
<div class="case-grid">
<div><p class="mini-label">The challenge</p><p>The challenge was measuring reaction time accurately while coordinating cue timing, player input, score, lives, false starts, and reset behavior within one control flow.</p></div>
<div><p class="mini-label">What I learned</p><p>This project gave me practical experience with timing and event-driven logic. Small errors in the control flow were immediately visible during testing, which helped me develop a more systematic approach to debugging interactive systems.</p></div>
<div class="case-built"><p class="mini-label">What I built</p><ol><li>LED and keypad-based sequence input</li><li>Timing logic for reaction measurements</li><li>Score, combo, life, and false-start tracking</li><li>Serial Monitor performance summaries</li></ol></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section aria-label="Ultrasonic Radar case study" class="case-modal" id="radar-case">
<a aria-label="Close case study" class="case-backdrop" href="#work"></a>
<div class="case-sheet">
<div class="case-topbar">
<p>04 / 05</p>
<a aria-label="Close" class="close-case" href="#work">×</a>
</div>
<div class="case-hero">
<div>
<p class="kicker">Sensing · scanning mechanism</p>
<h2>Ultrasonic Radar</h2>
<p class="case-lede">I mounted an ultrasonic sensor on a servo and swept it across different angles to measure distance and detect nearby objects.</p>
<div class="tag-row light"><span>HC-SR04</span><span>Servo</span><span>Distance Sensing</span><span>Scanning</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Frame from Ultrasonic Radar video" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="image-03.png"/>
</div>
<div class="case-grid">
<div><p class="mini-label">The challenge</p><p>The main challenge was making sure each distance reading matched the servo angle where it was actually measured.</p></div>
<div><p class="mini-label">What I learned</p><p>This project showed me how motion introduces additional timing and synchronization requirements. I learned to account for servo movement before treating each distance reading as valid.</p></div>
<div class="case-built"><p class="mini-label">What I built</p><ol><li>HC-SR04 distance sensing</li><li>Servo-driven sensor sweep</li><li>Repeated angle-and-distance sampling</li><li>Object detection across a scan range</li></ol></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section aria-label="Automatic Trash Can case study" class="case-modal" id="trashcan-case">
<a aria-label="Close case study" class="case-backdrop" href="#work"></a>
<div class="case-sheet">
<div class="case-topbar">
<p>05 / 05</p>
<a aria-label="Close" class="close-case" href="#work">×</a>
</div>
<div class="case-hero">
<div>
<p class="kicker">Automation · proximity sensing</p>
<h2>Automatic Trash Can</h2>
<p class="case-lede">I built a cardboard proof-of-concept that detects nearby motion with an ultrasonic sensor and uses a servo to open the lid automatically.</p>
<div class="tag-row light"><span>HC-SR04</span><span>Servo</span><span>Automation</span><span>Prototype</span></div>
</div>
<img alt="Frame from Automatic Trash Can video" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="image-05.png"/>
</div>
<div class="case-grid">
<div><p class="mini-label">The challenge</p><p>The challenge was turning imperfect distance readings into simple, reliable open/close behavior without making the lid trigger when it should not.</p></div>
<div><p class="mini-label">What I learned</p><p>This project reinforced how strongly software thresholds, sensor placement, and actuator direction influence the behavior and reliability of a physical system.</p></div>
<div class="case-built"><p class="mini-label">What I built</p><ol><li>Ultrasonic proximity detection</li><li>Servo-actuated lid mechanism</li><li>Near/far threshold logic</li><li>Physical prototype and motion debugging</li></ol></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<script>
(() => {
  const progress=document.getElementById('progress');
  const glow=document.getElementById('glow');
  const onScroll=()=>{const max=document.documentElement.scrollHeight-innerHeight;progress.style.width=(max>0?scrollY/max*100:0)+'%';};
  addEventListener('scroll',onScroll,{passive:true});onScroll();
  addEventListener('pointermove',e=>{glow.style.left=e.clientX+'px';glow.style.top=e.clientY+'px';},{passive:true});
  const observer=new IntersectionObserver(entries=>entries.forEach(e=>{if(e.isIntersecting)e.target.classList.add('visible');}),{threshold:.1});
  document.querySelectorAll('.reveal').forEach(el=>observer.observe(el));
  document.querySelectorAll('.project-card').forEach(card=>{
    const v=card.querySelector('video');
    card.addEventListener('mouseenter',()=>{ if(v && v.paused && !v.controls) v.play().catch(()=>{}); });
  });
  document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='Escape' && location.hash.includes('-case')) location.hash='work';});
})();
</script>
</body>
